  "answer": "Laplace transforms aren't covered in your Calculus I materials — they belong to a later course, so I can't ground an explanation in anything from this course.\n\nNot from your course materials: a Laplace transform takes a function f(t) and produces a new function F(s) defined by an integral, roughly F(s) = ∫₀^∞ e^(−st) f(t) dt, which turns derivatives into algebra and is used to solve differential equations. It relies on improper integrals and integration techniques you'll meet later.\n\nFor now, the closest Calculus I skill is setting up and evaluating definite integrals, so that's the groundwork worth solidifying. Which integration technique from this course feels least secure to you?",
